<!--Post: 4549--><!--Time: 1106517070-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4549"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 1:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wade, <BR> <BR>I am extremely limited on my time right now as I have already missed a writing deadline.  Could you provide the link?  Please? <BR> <BR>I will say, that&#39;s great work.  I had no idea about nsteam trains.  I think you&#39;re on to something.  If I were WPD, and I&#39;m hoping they already have, I would visit the train stores within Wichita and get a list of their long-time customers.  I would think he woudl visit the shop frequently. <BR> <BR>Thanks for providing the information.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4549-->
<!--Post: 4556--><!--Time: 1106519363-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4556"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Researcher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Researcher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 2:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I made mention on another board a while back that no one commented on. See the puzzle to see what you think - the one that has the word details in the sixth column <BR> <BR>Across - ?IAMCLOLDDL <BR>Down from the M-  RNLT     <BR> <BR>Maybe I am stretching it - could be I AM RN  &#40;registered nurse? or =his initials?&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4556-->
<!--Post: 4557--><!--Time: 1106519644-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4557"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 2:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That&#39;s interesting researcher.  I will go back and look. <BR> <BR>I have not yet taken the time to look at words formed by breaking the traditional rules of crosswords, as in boggle type words. <BR> <BR>Interesting.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4557-->
<!--Post: 4573--><!--Time: 1106536631-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4573"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 7:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jrl - a lot of work was done on the puzzles on other boards, and is found there, and is also summarized on another thread here &#40;see the links below&#41;.  It&#39;s worth taking a full evening and reviewing these threads.  These folks did an incredible amount of fabulous work, mostly based on images they had to capture from TV.  It&#39;s a true hacker war story. <BR> <BR>Researcher - You may be onto something.  I&#39;m glad I&#39;m not the only one intrigued by that line.  I noticed the &#34;IAM&#34; line as well, but I was reading it a bit differently, and got some interesting results.  Not to say that I AM RN isn&#39;t a very good possibility - he has other right-angle word-finds in the puzzle, and hospital-worker serial killers are almost a class in themselves.  But I&#39;m tempted to read it straight across. <BR> <BR>Here is my analysis.  For explanation, I refer to two items: &#40;1&#41; the <a href="http://crimeandjustice.i8.com/btkltr4WM.jpg">original screen capture</a>, and &#40;2&#41; Wichitaboy&#39;s theory of puzzle reconstruction, which is in &#40;among other places&#41; the earlier <a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/1/197.html?1105407138">Puzzles </a>thread.  All of this is built on the work of many people on various boards, who did a lot of work collecting and analyzing these puzzle images. <BR> <BR>Wichitaboy&#39;s idea is that the main and obvious MO-type puzzle words are just chaff.  Not only that - he identified many keyboard-adjacent letter sequences &#40;up/down, left/right&#41; that are presumably ALSO chaff.  The great thing about this idea is that it narrows down the number of letters that have a high likelihood of being EXCLUSIVELY related to any potential hidden message, as shown by white-backgrounded letters in Wichitaboy&#39;s <a href="http://pages.prodigy.net/cross/puz915moa.JPG">color-coded table</a>. <BR> <BR>Note that the IAM&#40;something&#41; sequence falls squarely in the &#34;high-value&#34; area.  If you go back to the original TV screen capture you will see that the full line, located at Row 9, is: <BR> <BR>Q I A M C L D D L <BR> <BR>Normally I would just blow CLDDL off as random except for several things: &#40;1&#41; it&#39;s high-value stuff, &#40;2&#41; BTK intentionally left an obvious &#34;I AM&#34; in the text - he could have easily removed it, but he didn&#39;t, &#40;3&#41; BTK&#39;s puzzle preferences, and &#40;4&#41; his desire to taunt.  In regard to item &#40;3&#41;, it appears that BTK&#39;s working crypto level in this case is word-finds, jumbles, buried words, etc.  Even assuming the BTK posers in &#34;From BTK&#34; are actually BTK, people seem to be finding solutions without resorting to NSA-level stuff.  Besides, what fun is it to send people stuff they don&#39;t get?  That&#39;s where item &#40;4&#41; comes in.  I think he wanted to stick it in LE&#39;s face.  Pure and simple.  Something that he will always be able to point to - even after capture - and say &#34;Look!  I even gave them my friggin&#39; name and they didn&#39;t see it!  I&#39;m sooooooo superior.&#34;  Unfortunately for BTK, I&#39;ll bet they did. <BR> <BR>I think that, rather than entrusting precious information to a code or cipher that he is not fully &#34;in control&#34; of, BTK precisely set the level of his maximum exposure at a &#34;first name confession&#34; - something safe that would never hold up in court.  Something along the lines of &#34;I am called Joe&#34;.  Then he just removed letters until it had the right hide/taunt/thrill/risk level, and also fit onto his spreadsheet.  Don&#39;t put it all the way on the left where it&#39;s too obvious.  Then add perpendicular intersecting chaff words, and away he goes, as per Wichitaboy&#39;s description.  So what would it mean?: <BR> <BR>Q  I  AM  CLD  DL <BR> <BR>My guess would be that CLD is either &#34;called&#34; or &#34;cold&#34; with vowels and duplicates removed.  BTK is possibly multilingual, so he may well be familiar with languages that omit vowels and/or duplicates in writing, plus it&#39;s a standard word puzzle trick.  I was leaning toward &#34;called&#34; earlier, but &#34;cold&#34; takes on new meaning with Tom&#39;s cryptic reference to ice trays, and all the running faucet stuff.  But what&#39;s DL?  Maybe it&#39;s like PJ - first/last or first/middle initials.  But it could also be a first name, like Dale, with the vowels removed.  Unfortunately, that&#39;s a lot of names, if you care to dig through them all.  I did.  Consider &#42;D&#42;L&#42; where &#42; = any combination of vowels or duplicated consonants D or L.  Ouch.  But it might be worth bumping those names up the suspect list - again, assuming it&#39;s not random junk and I&#39;m not reading too much into it. <BR> <BR>One final note - the very next line: <BR> <BR>W O S R T T B L S <BR> <BR>The high-value part &#40;SRTTBLS&#41;, if you add back vowels, is &#34;SORT TABLES&#34;, which is kinda what we just did.  Confirmation or coincidence?  Is it real, or is it just Memorex?  Who knows.  That&#39;s all that I can see.  As usual, BTK brings plenty of rope, but never enough to hang himself.  Even if I&#39;m right, he&#39;ll skate on it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4573-->
<!--Post: 4575--><!--Time: 1106538766-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4575"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 7:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thank you Pax.  I don&#39;t know how I missed those before.  I feel silly for starting this thread now.   <BR> <BR>However, I do feel the words I listed above, which may have seemed irrelevant at the time are relevant.  I agree with what others said on the other thread.  The obvious words that would seem like clues are nothing but distractors to take your eye away from the others.  It will be interesting to see if BTK is caught if he will clue us in on these puzzles.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4575-->
<!--Post: 4576--><!--Time: 1106540459-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4576"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 8:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Actually, Jrl, this area &#40;BTK Letters&#41; is where the thread probably should have been, although absent a Puzzles area, General BTK Discussion was also a decent choice.  I didn&#39;t find that thread either, until weeks after I started lurking!  Following the thread on another board where they got the tables from the screen captures was a dynamite read - even the soft flames between correspondents were fascinating.  In particular, one participant &#40;Miss Calculation - great name!&#41; was finding a lot of amazing embedded words by loosening constraints on word directions &#40;i.e., allowing twisting, turning, etc.&#41;.  Unfortunately, this was mostly just a probablility effect, unless BTK were using some sort of software to force higher layers of embedding along with his obvious words - which I think is unlikely &#40;although some of the very latest wordfind games do just that sort of thing&#41;.  As you say, it will be interesting once he&#39;s caught.... <BR> <BR>Yes, your systematic approach above has yielded some good stuff.  One thing that jumped out at me was &#34;ice&#34; and &#34;iceman&#34;.  There appears to be emerging info about BTK doing something nasty with ice or cold water.  Although &#34;iceman&#34; is really part of &#34;serviceman&#34;, I would bet heavily that BTK was quite aware of the embedded word, and was doing his taunt thing.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
