<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10103"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 2:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;ve been thinking throughout the re-emergence of BTK that part of the problem with dealing with a guy like Rader is that there is no word to describe him.  <BR> <BR>Mean really means mediocre, cruel implies power and control, evil, accurate but overused, dangerous is almost flattering.  <BR> <BR>In a world of terrorists, we need a really good gutteral sounding insult to depict the type of person who is a threat and morally bankrupt, but expresses it in a cowardly way, especially for selfish reasons. Something that doesn&#39;t imply that the recipient is fearsome or powerful, just capable of doing harm. <BR> <BR> <BR>If anyone knows such a word, in any language, I would appreciate them posting it here.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10103-->
<!--Post: 10195--><!--Time: 1109807773-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10195"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Marga<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Marga<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 3:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Good thought, Bmodule! A different word &#42;is&#42; needed. I&#39;ll start thinking... Meanwhile, another possible thing we could do is coin a &#42;new&#42; term or phrase to refer to such a person &#40;who is a threat/coward/evil/selfish&#41;--just create one ourselves.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10195-->
<!--Post: 10248--><!--Time: 1109840843-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10248"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 03, 2005 - 1:07 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I thought about that too. In fact I thought about that first...by incorporating BTK&#39;s name Rader into some type of spoonerism, but decided that would be unfair to the Raders of the world who are not murders. You notice that none of the Arnolds ever name their kids Benedict...or anyone else for that matter.   <BR> <BR>Iwas hoping that someone knew Japanese or any other language, or especially one of the Native American languages that had already tackled the problem. The more complicated the language, it seems, the more elegant and exacting the insults. <BR> <BR>It just seem that the Japanese are historically obsessed with exacting protocal and ettiquette and when describing an enemy would be very careful not to give them the satisfaction of implying that they held power of any type. <BR> <BR>Say for instance &#34;xxxxx&#34; meant: you are a mosquito. Small, insignificant, and a parasite that I am only aware of because of the lethal disease that you may carry and the pest that you are. <BR> <BR>Then BTK would be an &#34;xxxxx&#34;. <BR> <BR>Just think how that would have messed with his &#34;control console&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10248-->
<!--Post: 10293--><!--Time: 1109879323-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10293"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tucker<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tucker<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 03, 2005 - 11:48 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is from Pax56, posted right here on 1/23/05.. <BR>Q I AM CLD DL <BR> <BR>My guess would be that CLD is either &#34;called&#34; or &#34;cold&#34; with vowels and duplicates removed. BTK is possibly multilingual, so he may well be familiar with languages that omit vowels and/or duplicates in writing, plus it&#39;s a standard word puzzle trick. I was leaning toward &#34;called&#34; earlier, but &#34;cold&#34; takes on new meaning with Tom&#39;s cryptic reference to ice trays, and all the running faucet stuff. But what&#39;s DL? Maybe it&#39;s like PJ - first/last or first/middle initials. But it could also be a first name, like Dale, with the vowels removed. Unfortunately, that&#39;s a lot of names, if you care to dig through them all. I did. Consider &#42;D&#42;L&#42; where &#42; = any combination of vowels or duplicated consonants D or L. Ouch. But it might be worth bumping those names up the suspect list - again, assuming it&#39;s not random junk and I&#39;m not reading too much into it.  <BR> <BR>Talk about basically hitting the nail on the head, as DL could easily be Dennis Lynn.  Not sure if people can verify this, but maybe his friends did call him D.L.  Just thought I would point out Pax&#39;s good work.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
